---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Glance from "../../components/Glance.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="scorecard-gamification"
  team="PM, EM & engineering (incl. architect)"
  readTime="7 min read"
  coverPlain
>
  <Glance>
    <Fragment slot="challenge">
      Of 117 customers with active Scorecards, only ~28% engaged enough to move
      the needle. Compliance managers couldn't say which rules mattered most, and
      engineers had no reason to care. Leadership's brief was a single word:
      gamification.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="strategy">
      Interrogate "gamification" into concrete mechanics, then invent a leveling
      model that fits New Relic's inverted data model without breaking the
      flexibility that makes it different.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="impact">
      A shipped leveling, weights, and leaderboard system — anchored by a
      visibility matrix that kept the game metaphor from leaking across the
      product.
    </Fragment>
  </Glance>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 sm:px-10">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Scorecards is how New Relic customers hold their engineering organization
        to a bar — quality, security, ownership, production readiness. But of the
        117 customers with active Scorecards, only 33 (≈28%) were engaging with
        them enough to move the needle. Compliance managers had no way to say
        <em>this rule matters more than that one</em>. Engineers had no reason to
        care about the scoreboard. Leadership handed the design team a single
        word: <strong>gamification</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        I was the product designer. My work started with a competitive analysis,
        ended with a shipped feature, and — in the middle — required inventing an
        entire IA on top of a data model that made the obvious answer
        unavailable.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </section>

  <CaseSection num="01" title="The word I couldn't take at face value">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        "Gamification" is a solution word disguised as a problem. My first
        instinct on receiving the brief was not to design — it was to decide
        whether the word was the right one, and if so, which of its mechanics
        actually mapped to our users, our data, and our system.
      </p>
      <p>
        So before I opened Figma, I built a competitive deck for the review with
        the VP of Product, VP of Engineering, Senior Design Director, PM, and EM.
        The deck opened not with a screen, but with a business frame:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Business value:</strong> Close the competitive gap with Cortex.io.
        Advanced CCU. Retention against a 28% adoption gap. One of the building
        blocks of New Relic's observability intelligence bet.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Customer value:</strong> Uptime. Operational excellence. MTTR.
        Resource allocation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Setting the frame in the language senior stakeholders used to make funding
        decisions — before I made a single design claim — was deliberate. It meant
        every design trade-off later in the review carried a defensible line back
        to something the room already agreed was worth solving.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <div
      class="my-8 flex min-h-[220px] items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-6 text-center"
    >
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        [ image — deck: business value slide ]
      </p>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="02" title="Decomposing gamification into mechanics">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        "Gamification" as a single concept is unfalsifiable. But it decomposes
        cleanly into three mechanics, and each one can be interrogated on its own:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Leveling</strong> — do users progress through named tiers
          (Bronze → Silver → Gold)?</li>
        <li><strong>Leaderboard</strong> — do teams or entities rank against each
          other?</li>
        <li><strong>Weights</strong> — can a user say <em>this rule matters
          more</em>?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I benchmarked six competitors — Compass, OpsLevel, Rely.io, Cortex, Port,
        and Datadog — against each mechanic. Every competitor except Datadog had
        adopted at least the first two. But the pattern they were using rested on
        an assumption I hadn't seen anyone in the room name yet.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <div
      class="my-8 flex min-h-[220px] items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-6 text-center"
    >
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        [ image — deck: comparison matrices across the three mechanics ]
      </p>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="03" title="The constraint no one had named">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Every competitor's leveling pattern works because their entities live at
        the scorecard level. A service belongs to Cortex's Production Readiness
        scorecard; it moves through Bronze → Silver → Gold within that scorecard.
        The level is an attribute of the entity, scoped to one scorecard. Simple.
      </p>
      <p>New Relic's model is inverted.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A Scorecard contains multiple Rules.</li>
        <li>Each Rule applies to a set of Entities (a service, a browser app,
          whatever).</li>
        <li>An entity is not "in" a scorecard the way it is in Cortex. It's in the
          intersection of a scorecard's rules.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That inversion was a feature — it's why customers can compose rules
        flexibly, why the same entity can be evaluated against different criteria
        in different scorecards. But it meant the competitor pattern was
        structurally unavailable to us. Copying Cortex would have required
        rebuilding the data model.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Callout label="The load-bearing question">
      Can we give users the mental model of leveling up without breaking the data
      model that makes New Relic Scorecards different?
    </Callout>
    <div
      class="my-8 flex min-h-[220px] items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-6 text-center"
    >
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        [ diagram — competitor IA (entity → scorecard) vs. NR IA (entity → rule →
        scorecard) ]
      </p>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="04" title="Inventing the mechanism">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        I worked with the PM and Engineering — including the team's architect — to
        invent the pattern. The move was structurally simple in hindsight, but it
        required rejecting the competitor pattern deliberately rather than by
        default.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Levels don't belong to scorecards. They belong to <strong>rules within
          a scorecard</strong>.</li>
        <li>Each rule in a scorecard is assigned to a level (default: Below Basic,
          Basic, Mature, Advanced; customizable).</li>
        <li>An entity's achieved level in a scorecard is the highest level whose
          rules the entity is passing.</li>
        <li>Weights are configurable per rule within a scorecard, so a compliance
          manager can also express <em>this rule matters more</em> independently
          of the level system.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The mechanism preserved New Relic's flexibility. But it introduced a new
        problem: now the concept of "level" could appear almost anywhere — the
        Scorecards main page, a Scorecard's detail page, the Teams view, the
        Catalogs — at three different granularities: Entity, Rule, and Scorecard.
        That's twelve possible intersections, and showing levels at all twelve
        would drown the interface. So the mechanism needed a rule of visibility.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="my-8 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-line">
            <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Surface</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Entity</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Rule</th>
            <th class="py-3 pl-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Scorecard</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="align-top text-ink/85">
          <tr class="border-b border-line/60">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Scorecards main page</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 text-muted/50">—</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 text-muted/50">—</td>
            <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-muted/50">—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-line/60">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Scorecard page</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Distribution of entities across rules</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">The level the rule belongs to</td>
            <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-muted/50">—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-line/60">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Teams view</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 text-muted/50">—</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 text-muted/50">—</td>
            <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-muted/50">—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Catalogs</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 text-muted/50">—</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Progress across rules</td>
            <td class="py-3 pl-4">The level the entity has achieved</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <Prose>
      <p>
        This table is the design artifact I brought into the second review. Not a
        mockup — a decision. Every "—" is a deliberate refusal. Levels don't
        appear on the main scorecards list, because the list is about breadth and
        status; leveling there would make browsing feel like judgment. They don't
        appear in the Teams view, because Teams already has its own ranking and
        medals; a second axis would fight the first. They appear in Catalogs at
        the entity → scorecard intersection, because that's where an engineer
        working on a specific service asks "how am I doing?" — the exact question
        the leveling metaphor answers.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Callout>
      The matrix is the reason the leveling metaphor survived contact with the
      rest of the product. Without it, the concept would have leaked into every
      surface and produced the "everything is a game" feeling that gamification is
      rightly criticized for.
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="05" title="The other question — who wins the tournament?">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Levels and weights answered <em>how does an individual entity or team make
        progress</em>. The leaderboard asked a different question: when we
        recognize a team as this month's winner, what exactly are we recognizing?
      </p>
      <p>
        The obvious answer was "count the medals," Olympic-style. But it had a
        structural flaw. Teams don't have identical scorecard portfolios — one
        team might own five scorecards, another two. A raw medal count wouldn't
        compare engineering excellence; it would compare scorecard adoption.
        That's the unequal footing problem. I laid out three mental models with
        explicit failure modes, and brought all three to the review:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Proposal 1 — Olympic (scorecard-level badging).</strong> Award
          badges per scorecard, aggregate per team. Preserves rich per-scorecard
          signal. Breaks on unequal footing across teams.</li>
        <li><strong>Proposal 2 — Team-level only.</strong> Compute one overall team
          score, map it to a universal badge tier. Every team graded on the same
          scale. Loses per-scorecard granularity.</li>
        <li><strong>Proposal 3 — Hybrid.</strong> Badges per scorecard for
          individual progression, but ranking uses overall team score for the
          tournament. Preserves granularity and fixes footing — at the cost of a
          new confusion: the badges a user sees don't directly explain the team's
          tournament position.</li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
    <div
      class="my-8 flex min-h-[220px] items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-6 text-center"
    >
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
        [ image — the three-proposal exploration artifact ]
      </p>
    </div>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The room chose Proposal 2 — the simplest one. And the meta-move here is
        the part that separates this from a "we all agreed" story: my job wasn't
        to advocate for a proposal.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Callout label="The meta-move">
      My job wasn't to advocate for a proposal. It was to make the trade-offs
      legible enough that the room could pick with confidence.
    </Callout>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Proposal 2 won because complexity was already high elsewhere — levels,
        weights, the visibility matrix — and the room had a cognitive-load bar to
        protect. If I'd walked in with Proposal 3 as "the right answer," stacking a
        fourth mental model on top of three others would have surfaced as pushback
        in the next review, or worse, in production. Instead, we shipped Proposal 2
        with the criteria written down. If a future team ever wants per-scorecard
        badging back, they know exactly what they're trading.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="06" title="Convergence, not consensus">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The proposal went through three design reviews with VP-level stakeholders.
        The first pushed back on complexity — even after the badging
        simplification, leveling plus weights plus the visibility matrix was more
        than the room wanted a compliance manager to hold at once. I iterated. The
        second resolved most of the complexity concerns but surfaced a question
        about how weights and levels interact on the entity's final score. I
        iterated again. The third was buy-in.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each iteration wasn't capitulation. It was a judgment call about whether
        the pushback was pointing at a real design failure — genuine cognitive
        overload — or at a scoping concern I needed to defend. My job in those
        three rounds was not to convince the VPs; it was to converge the room on
        the right complexity bar.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <aside
      class="my-8 rounded-xl border border-dashed border-[#0041d9]/40 bg-[#0041d9]/[0.04] px-5 py-4"
    >
      <p class="mb-1 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-[#0041d9]">
        Draft note — to fill in
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Name one specific piece of feedback you disagreed with and either pushed
        back on or absorbed with modification — the "receipts" moment that
        separates "I iterated on feedback" from "I made judgment calls." Even one
        sentence lands.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="07" title="Craft">
    <aside
      class="my-8 rounded-xl border border-dashed border-[#0041d9]/40 bg-[#0041d9]/[0.04] px-5 py-4"
    >
      <p class="mb-1 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-[#0041d9]">
        Draft note — to fill in (keep short)
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        3–5 Figma frames + production gifs showing how the visibility matrix
        translated to real UI: scorecard detail page with rule/level tags, catalog
        progression drawer, weights configuration flow, team leaderboard with
        medal badging.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="my-8 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div
        class="flex min-h-[200px] items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-6 text-center"
      >
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
          [ image — scorecard detail: rule/level tags ]
        </p>
      </div>
      <div
        class="flex min-h-[200px] items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-6 text-center"
      >
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
          [ image — catalog progression drawer ]
        </p>
      </div>
      <div
        class="flex min-h-[200px] items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-6 text-center"
      >
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
          [ image — weights configuration flow ]
        </p>
      </div>
      <div
        class="flex min-h-[200px] items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed border-line bg-surface px-6 text-center"
      >
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">
          [ image — team leaderboard with medal badging ]
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="08" title="Outcomes and reflection">
    <aside
      class="my-8 rounded-xl border border-dashed border-[#0041d9]/40 bg-[#0041d9]/[0.04] px-5 py-4"
    >
      <p class="mb-1 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-[#0041d9]">
        Draft note — to fill in
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Post-launch metrics: adoption change vs. the 28% baseline, CCU signal, a
        customer or internal quote, deals cited. If there are no hard numbers yet,
        one directional line is fine. Then one honest "what I'd do differently" —
        e.g. a surface where the visibility rule should have differed in
        hindsight, or front-loading the constraint conversation with Eng earlier.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
